WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 18: Emperor Norton I: The Sovereign of San Francisco

1
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.200
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.480
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:13.760
[Rebecca] The United States of America was founded on the rejection of monarchy, fighting a revolution to overthrow King George I.I.I.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:20.640
[Rebecca] Yet, in the golden, eccentric streets of 19th century San Francisco, America had an emperor.

5
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.680
[Rebecca] His name was Joshua Abraham Norton.

6
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:41.840
[Rebecca] In 1859, after losing his entire fortune in a disastrous rice speculation scheme, Norton walked into the offices of the San Francisco Daily Evening Bulletin and published a formal proclamation declaring himself Norton I, Emperor of the United States and Protector of Mexico.

7
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.120
[Rebecca] Instead of locking him in an asylum, the city of San Francisco did something wonderful.

8
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.640
[Rebecca] They adopted him.

9
00:00:48.640 --> 00:01:01.040
[Rebecca] For 21 years, citizens bowed in the streets, police officers saluted his imperial uniform, restaurants accepted his hand printed imperial currency, and the city council funded his wardrobe.

10
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.520
[George] Here is George with the story.

11
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:21.280
[George] Born in England in 1818 and raised in South Africa, Norton arrived in San Francisco in 1849 during the thrilling chaos of the California Gold Rush.

12
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.720
[George] He possessed a sharp business acumen and $4,000 in family capital.

13
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:42.000
[George] While thousands of desperate prospectors starved in the goldfields, Norton grew wealthy investing in real estate, shipping, and food commodities, expanding his net worth to over a quarter of a million dollars, a colossal fortune in the 1850s.

14
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.120
[George] In 1852, however, Norton saw an opportunity for a monopoly that would prove to be his financial ruin.

15
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:59.840
[George] Famine in China caused the Chinese government to ban rice exports, driving the price of rice in San Francisco from four cents a pound to an astronomical 36 cents.

16
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:12.440
[George] When a Spanish ship arrived in San Francisco Harbor carrying 200,000 pounds of Peruvian rice, Norton bought the entire cargo for $25,000, believing he could corner the market.

17
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.680
[George] Almost immediately after signing the contract, disaster struck.

18
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:23.320
[George] Several other ships laden with Peruvian rice sailed unexpectedly through the Golden Gate, flooding the market.

19
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.440
[George] The price of rice crashed to three cents a pound.

20
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.600
[George] Norton was financially ruined.

21
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.600
[George] He spent years in bitter litigation attempting to void the contract, but lost every appeal.

22
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:42.920
[George] In 1858, he declared bankruptcy, lost his mansion, and vanished into the squalid boarding houses of San Francisco.

23
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:55.400
[George] Norton had previously operated an import-export firm on Sansome Street, building an impeccable reputation among San Francisco's merchant elite as a man of absolute honesty and financial integrity.

24
00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:03.080
[George] After months of complete seclusion, Joshua Norton re-emerged into public life with a fundamentally transformed identity.

25
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:10.440
[George] The financial trauma had broken his connection to ordinary reality, replacing it with a grand, benevolent delusion.

26
00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:20.040
[George] Norton concluded that the democratic institutions of the United States were hopelessly corrupt, paralyzed by factional hatred, and sliding toward civil war.

27
00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.960
[George] What the fractured nation required was a supreme, moral monarch to restore justice and harmony.

28
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:37.240
[George] On September 17, 1859, Norton walked into the editorial offices of the San Francisco Daily Evening Bulletin, handing a handwritten manifesto to the editor.

29
00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:42.120
[George] The editor, amused by the eccentric document, printed it on the front page.

30
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:58.240
[George] At the peremptory request and desire of a large majority of the citizens of these United States, I, Joshua Norton, formerly of Algoa Bay, Cape of Good Hope, and now for the last nine years and ten months past of San Francisco, California.

31
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] Declare and proclaim myself Emperor of these United States.

32
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:13.200
[George] Over the following years, as Mexico descended into the French intervention under Maximilian, Norton graciously expanded his title to Emperor of the United States and Protector of Mexico.

33
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.040
[George] America had its monarch.

34
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.160
[George] And the citizens of San Francisco were delighted.

35
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:29.040
[George] The editor of the bulletin later remarked that he printed Norton's declaration because it offered a refreshing, dignified contrast to the corrupt, self-serving political bickering of the era.

36
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.240
[George] Emperor Norton took his imperial duties with absolute, solemn seriousness.

37
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:51.120
[George] Every morning, he dressed in an elaborate military uniform gifted to him by officers at the Presidio Army base, a navy blue coat with heavy gold braided epaulets, a beaver top hat decorated with a peacock feather and a military rosette, and a polished wooden walking stick.

38
00:04:51.120 --> 00:05:07.680
[George] He spent his days patrolling the cobblestone streets of San Francisco on foot, inspecting public works projects, checking streetcar tracks, ensuring police officers were at their posts, and visiting construction sites to guarantee that public buildings met imperial standards.

39
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:16.800
[George] He was accompanied everywhere by two legendary stray dogs named Bummer and Lazarus, who were celebrated figures in San Francisco folklore.

40
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.960
[George] San Franciscans treated Norton with warm, affectionate deference.

41
00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:29.360
[George] When the emperor entered a theater, the entire audience rose to its feet, and the finest private box was reserved for him free of charge.

42
00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:39.880
[George] Restaurants and coffeehouses competed for the honor of having him dine at their tables, placing brass plaques in their windows reading, by appointment to his Imperial Majesty.

43
00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:50.840
[George] When his uniform grew threadbare, the San Francisco Board of Supervisors passed an official municipal resolution appropriating public funds to buy him a brand new imperial suit.

44
00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:57.880
[George] Norton sent gracious letters of thanks to the city council, granting them each an imperial patent of nobility.

45
00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:13.000
[George] Emperor Norton was a devout attendee of religious services, rotating his presence every week between Catholic Mass, Protestant Sunday sermons, and Jewish Saturday synagogue services to ensure all his subjects felt equal imperial favor.

46
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:19.160
[George] Emperor Norton ruled through a prolific stream of imperial decrees published in local newspapers.

47
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:32.920
[George] While many decrees were delightfully eccentric, such as formally abolishing the United States Congress and commanding General Winfield Scott to clear the capital with bayonets, many of his proclamations were astonishingly visionary.

48
00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:50.920
[George] In 1872, decades before modern engineering made it feasible, Norton issued an imperial decree commanding the construction of a suspension bridge and tunnel linking San Francisco directly to Oakland via Goat Island, ordering the city council to begin surveying immediately.

49
00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:59.880
[George] Sixty-four years later, the San Francisco Oakland Bay Bridge was constructed along the exact geographical alignment the Emperor had specified.

50
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:05.480
[George] Norton was also a fierce defender of minority rights in an era of intense racial prejudice.

51
00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:14.840
[George] He issued decrees demanding that African Americans be permitted to ride public streetcars and that Chinese immigrants be granted full legal protection in court.

52
00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:25.920
[George] During the violent anti-Chinese riots of the late 1870s, an angry white mob gathered to storm Chinatown, intending to lynch Chinese laborers and burn their homes.

53
00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:36.960
[George] Emperor Norton walked into the center of the street, stood directly between the armed mob and the Chinese enclave, bowed his head, and began reciting the Lord's Prayer over and over.

54
00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:45.120
[George] Ashamed by the dignity and moral authority of their beloved emperor, the rioters quietly dispersed without shedding a drop of blood.

55
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:56.000
[George] Norton's vision of a suspension bridge was so specific that his decree designated the exact engineering route from San Francisco through Yerba Buena Island to the shores of Oakland.

56
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:02.080
[George] Like any sovereign monarch, Emperor Norton required funds to support his royal administration.

57
00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:15.760
[George] Rather than begging, Norton issued his own imperial script, beautifully printed promissory notes bearing his portrait, signature and gold seal, in denominations of fifty cents, $1, and $10.

58
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:21.760
[George] Remarkably, Norton's imperial currency was universally accepted throughout San Francisco.

59
00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:29.760
[George] Merchants accepted the bills for food, tobacco and drinks, trading them among themselves or saving them as prized collectibles.

60
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:37.840
[George] Norton also levied modest imperial taxes on local businesses, typically 50 cents a month from banks and prominent merchants.

61
00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:44.960
[George] The businessmen cheerfully paid the assessments, and Norton scrupulously recorded every transaction in an imperial ledger.

62
00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:57.520
[George] When an overzealous rookie police officer named Armand Barbier arrested Emperor Norton in 1867, charging him with vagrancy and insanity, public outrage exploded across San Francisco.

63
00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:04.200
[George] Newspaper editorials denounced the arrest, declaring that the Emperor had harmed no man and shed no blood.

64
00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:18.200
[George] The chief of police, Patrick Crowley, immediately ordered Norton released, issued a formal public apology on behalf of the city, and commanded that all police officers salute Emperor Norton whenever he passed them on the street.

65
00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:25.000
[George] Norton, displaying true imperial magnanimity, granted the young officer a full imperial pardon.

66
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:37.720
[George] Prominent San Francisco printers, including Cuddy and Hughes, volunteered their presses to engrave the Emperor's banknotes free of charge, using high-quality parchment paper with intricate filigree borders.

67
00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:50.760
[George] On the rainy evening of January 8, 1880, Emperor Norton was walking to a lecture at the California Academy of Sciences when he suddenly collapsed on the sidewalk at the corner of California and DuPont streets.

68
00:09:50.760 --> 00:10:01.080
[George] A crowd of citizens rushed to his aid, and a police carriage carried him to the city hospital, but he died before doctors could treat him, having suffered a cerebral hemorrhage.

69
00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:19.000
[George] When police searched his humble, six by ten-foot room in a boarding house on Commercial Street, they found no hidden fortune, only a collection of walking sticks, a few French francs, sovereign bonds, and an assortment of affectionate letters from European monarchs, including Queen Victoria.

70
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:26.760
[George] The headline of the San Francisco Chronicle the following morning announced simply, Le Roy Est Mort, the king is dead.

71
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:35.320
[George] The Pacific Club, a prestigious association of wealthy San Francisco businessmen, paid for a lavish funeral with a rosewood casket.

72
00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:44.960
[George] On the day of his burial, over 30,000 people, more than 10% of the entire population of San Francisco, lined the streets to pay their respects.

73
00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:52.160
[George] The funeral procession stretched for two miles, uniting rich and poor, laborers and tycoons in profound grief.

74
00:10:52.400 --> 00:11:03.360
[George] Norton I was buried at Woodlawn Cemetery in Calma, under a massive granite headstone inscribed, Norton I, Emperor of the United States and Protector of Mexico.

75
00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:13.840
[George] He remains immortal, the mad, gentle monarch whose reign was sustained not by swords or armies, but by the boundless kindness and affection of an American city.

76
00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:25.600
[George] In 1980, on the centenary of his death, hundreds of San Francisco citizens gathered at his grave in Calma, holding an imperial memorial service to honor America's only emperor.

77
00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:41.520
[Rebecca] It stands as an enduring tribute to the city of San Francisco, proving that compassion, whimsy, and community can turn an impoverished madman into a beloved monarch.

78
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.480
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

79
00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:45.680
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

80
00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.040
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

81
00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.440
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

